+---
+id: dl-001-resolvepointer-strictness-own-properties-only-errors-for-unresolvable-pointers
+type: decision-log
+title: "resolvePointer strictness: own properties only, errors for unresolvable pointers"
+status: pending
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold verbatim; `memory submit` replaces
+     these placeholder comments with real content and fills the required frontmatter fields. -->
+
+# Context
+
+Later features build on `resolvePointer`, and other services import it, so it must follow RFC 6901
+exactly. The RFC leaves some outcomes to the application: what an unresolvable pointer does.
+
+# Decision
+
+- Invalid syntax (not empty and not starting with `/`, or `~` not followed by `0` or `1`) throws.
+- A pointer that does not resolve throws: missing member, array index out of range, `-` (which names
+  the nonexistent element after the last), or a step into a primitive or `null`.
+- Array indexes must be canonical (`0` or digits without a leading zero or sign).
+- Only own members of objects are looked up, so `/constructor`, `/__proto__` or `/length` never reach
+  inherited properties.
+- A member whose value is `null` or falsy resolves normally; only absence throws.
+
+# Consequences
+
+- Callers can rely on a returned value being really in the document, and catch the error otherwise.
+- No "not found" sentinel exists; features needing optional lookup must catch the error.
+- `-` is rejected for reading; a future write feature (e.g. JSON Patch add) will need its own handling.
